    options: null,
    answer: null,
    solution: "Continuous integration is the practice of merging everyone's work into a shared mainline *frequently* (at least daily) and **automatically verifying each change** so that integration problems are caught immediately, while they're small, rather than accumulating into a painful 'merge hell' at the end of a project. The automation is the CI **pipeline**: on every push (and every pull request), a CI server checks out the code and runs a series of gates — typically: **install dependencies reproducibly** (from the lockfile), **build/compile** (catch syntax and type errors), **run the linter and formatter checks** (style and obvious-bug rules), **run the test suite** (unit and integration tests), and often **security scans** (dependency vulnerabilities, secret detection) and code-coverage checks. The whole thing must be **fast** (minutes, not hours — slow CI is ignored CI) and report a clear pass/fail back on the PR (the red X / green check). The mechanism underneath is exit codes: each step exits non-zero on failure, failing the job, blocking the merge. Why 'keep the build green' is near-sacred to good teams: (1) **a green main is a deployable main** — at any moment you can ship, which underpins continuous delivery and fast incident response; (2) **a red build blocks *everyone*** — if main is broken, every teammate who pulls inherits the breakage and can't trust their own test results, so a broken build is a *team-wide* outage of productivity, not one person's problem; (3) **fast feedback keeps fixes cheap** — a test that fails 3 minutes after your push is fixed while the change is fresh in your head; the same bug found in staging next week is an archaeology project; (4) it **enforces shared standards automatically** — nobody argues about style in review because the linter already did, and nobody merges untested code because the gate won't let them. The cultural norms that go with it: **fix a broken build immediately** (a red main is everyone's top priority — revert if you can't fix fast), **don't merge on red**, and **run tests locally before pushing** so you're not using CI as your first test run. This connects to the broader CI/CD pipeline: CI (integrate + verify) feeds **continuous delivery** (every green build is *deployable*, deploy is a button) and **continuous deployment** (every green build deploys to production automatically). Interview tip: 'CI = integrate frequently + automatically build, lint, and test every change so problems surface small and early; keep main green because a broken build blocks the whole team and a deployable mainline is the goal' is the complete answer; mentioning fast feedback and 'fix red builds immediately' shows you've worked in a team that lived it."
