    question: "What is the YAGNI principle, and which scenario shows it being applied wisely?",
    code: null,
    options: {
      a: "YAGNI ('You Aren't Gonna Need It') means delete all error handling because errors rarely happen",
      b: "YAGNI says don't build functionality until it's actually needed — so a team building a single-currency app for one country wisely skips the multi-currency, multi-locale abstraction layer 'for when we go global', and instead builds the simplest thing that solves today's problem, adding generality when (and if) a real requirement arrives",
      c: "YAGNI means you never need to write tests",
      d: "YAGNI requires deleting any code you didn't write yourself"
    },
    answer: "b",
    solution: "Correct: b. YAGNI — 'You Aren't Gonna Need It' — is the principle that you should build functionality **when you actually need it, not when you merely anticipate needing it**. It targets *speculative generality*: the abstraction layers, configuration options, plugin systems, and 'flexible' frameworks built for imagined future requirements that, statistically, mostly never arrive — or arrive in a *different* shape than you guessed, so the speculative work has to be torn out anyway. The cost of building-ahead is real and triple: (1) the **time** spent building (and testing, and documenting) features nobody uses yet, which is time not spent on what's needed *now*; (2) the **ongoing complexity tax** — that multi-currency abstraction makes *every* future change harder to reason about, even though it's serving one currency; speculative flexibility is carried as dead weight on every read and every modification; and (3) the **wrong-guess risk** — you usually don't yet understand the future requirement well enough to design the right abstraction for it (the same insight behind the Rule of Three and 'the wrong abstraction is costlier than duplication'). So the wise move is to **build the simplest thing that solves today's concrete problem**, keep the code clean and changeable, and add the generality *when a real, present requirement forces it* — at which point you'll understand it well enough to build it right. The single-currency example is textbook: don't build the multi-currency/multi-locale machinery on a *maybe*; build for one currency cleanly, and when the company actually expands, build it *then* with real requirements in hand.\n\nThe important balance (so you don't sound reckless): YAGNI is about not building speculative *features and abstractions*, **not** an excuse to skip fundamentals that you *do* need now — error handling, tests, security, and basic good structure are needed *today*, so options a and c misread it badly (and d is unrelated). And YAGNI doesn't forbid *thinking ahead* or making the code easy to change later; it forbids *prematurely building* the thing. There's also genuine judgment in where to draw the line — some architectural decisions are expensive to reverse (database choice, public API contracts) and warrant more upfront thought than a feature flag. Interview tip: 'don't build it until a real requirement needs it, because speculative generality costs build time, ongoing complexity, and is usually the wrong guess — but YAGNI is about features/abstractions, not an excuse to skip tests, error handling, or security you need now' is the complete, balanced answer that shows you can apply it without weaponizing it."
