    code: "function proc(d) {\n  let r = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < d.length; i++) {\n    if (d[i].s === 1 && d[i].a > 18) {\n      let t = d[i].p * 0.9;\n      r.push({ ...d[i], p: t });\n    }\n  }\n  return r;\n}",
    options: null,
    answer: null,
    solution: "The function *works*, but it's nearly unreadable, and the fixes are all about **communicating intent** — to the next developer, who is most often future-you. Without changing behavior:\n\n**1. Naming.** `proc`, `d`, `r`, `t`, `s`, `a`, `p` are meaningless single letters and abbreviations — the reader has to reverse-engineer what `s === 1` and `a > 18` *mean*. Names are the cheapest, highest-impact documentation there is. Reverse-engineering from context (status === active, age > 18, price), a far clearer version:\n\nfunction applyAdultDiscount(customers) {\n  return customers\n    .filter((c) => c.status === ACTIVE && c.age > 18)\n    .map((c) => ({ ...c, price: c.price * 0.9 }));\n}\n\n**2. Magic numbers.** `1`, `18`, and `0.9` are unexplained constants — the reader can't tell *why* 0.9 (a 10% discount) or what status `1` is. Replace with named constants: `const ACTIVE = 1;`, `const ADULT_AGE = 18;`, `const DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.1;` (then `price * (1 - DISCOUNT_RATE)`), so the *meaning* is in the code and changing the discount is a one-line edit in an obvious place.\n\n**3. Structure — express intent over mechanics.** The manual `for` loop with index, a conditional, and a push is *imperative plumbing* that obscures what's happening. `filter` + `map` declares the intent directly: 'keep the active adults, then apply the discount.' The reader sees *what*, not *how*. (This is a readability choice, not a behavior change — same output.)\n\nWhy this matters so much in a *team* codebase, which is the real point: **code is read far more often than it's written** — this function will be read dozens of times (during reviews, debugging, feature changes) for every once it's modified, so optimizing for the reader pays compounding dividends. Cryptic code forces every future reader to re-derive intent, multiplying that cost across the whole team and the codebase's lifetime; clear names and structure are a form of documentation that *can't* go stale (unlike comments). And **small, well-named functions** that do one thing are easier to test, reuse, and reason about than long ones — function size and naming are load-bearing, not cosmetic. The caveat to keep it non-dogmatic: don't over-engineer a throwaway script, and sometimes a comment explaining a genuinely non-obvious *why* still belongs. Interview tip: naming the three improvements — descriptive names, named constants instead of magic numbers, and declarative filter/map structure — and grounding it in 'code is read far more than written, so I optimize for the next reader' is exactly the maintainability-minded answer reviewers reward."
