    question: "A production bug is causing data corruption right now. The team uses a normal multi-step release process (PR, review, CI, staging, scheduled deploy). What's the appropriate response, and what should follow?",
    code: null,
    options: {
      a: "Wait for the next scheduled release so the normal process is respected",
      b: "Push a fix directly to production manually, skipping all process, and don't tell anyone to avoid alarm",
      c: "Follow the team's hotfix/incident process: stop the bleeding fast (a targeted fix or rollback / feature-flag off), get an expedited review even if abbreviated, deploy via the hotfix path, then afterward write a blameless postmortem and add a regression test so it can't recur",
      d: "Rewrite the entire affected module properly before deploying anything"
    },
    answer: "c",
    solution: "Correct: c. Active data corruption is an **incident**, and incidents have a different (but still disciplined) process from normal feature work — the priority order is **stop the bleeding first, fix properly second, prevent recurrence third**. Concretely: (1) **Mitigate immediately** — the fastest *safe* action to halt the damage, which is often *not* a code fix at all but a **rollback** to the last known-good version or flipping a **feature flag off** to disable the broken path (instant, low-risk, no new code to get wrong under pressure); if a forward fix is needed, make it the *smallest targeted change* that stops the corruption, not a broad refactor. (2) **Use the hotfix path with appropriate (abbreviated but real) oversight** — most teams have an expedited process: a hotfix branch, an *urgent* review (a second pair of eyes even if quick — pressure is exactly when mistakes happen, so you don't skip review entirely), CI still runs, and a fast deploy. The process is *compressed*, not *abandoned*. (3) **Communicate** — tell the team/stakeholders; incidents are a team event, and hiding them (option b) prevents others from helping, removes oversight, and destroys trust. (4) **Afterward: blameless postmortem + regression test** — once stable, write up what happened, *why* (root cause, not who to blame — blameless culture is what makes people surface problems instead of hiding them), and the action items, and critically **add an automated test that reproduces the bug** so the same regression can never silently return. This last step is what turns an incident into a permanent improvement.\n\nWhy the others are wrong: a — waiting for the scheduled release while data corrupts is unacceptable; the *point* of a hotfix process is that severity dictates urgency, and 'respecting process' doesn't mean ignoring severity. b — manually pushing with *zero* review and *hiding it* is the reckless extreme: it removes the safety check exactly when error-proneness is highest, leaves no record, and betrays the team; mitigating *fast* doesn't mean mitigating *blind and secret*. d — a full proper rewrite is the *right* eventual fix but the *wrong* emergency response: it takes too long while data corrupts, and big changes under pressure are how you cause a *second* incident; stabilize first, do the thorough fix as planned follow-up work.\n\nThe maturity this question probes: knowing that emergencies warrant a *different, faster* process — not *no* process — and that the work isn't done when the fire's out (postmortem + regression test + the real fix come after). Interview tip: 'mitigate fast (rollback or flag off), abbreviated-but-real review via the hotfix path, communicate, then blameless postmortem with a regression test' is exactly the incident-response answer; emphasizing rollback-over-forward-fix and 'blameless' signals real operational experience."
